{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793032748,"nonce":"0bPz1EAe3I4EgTRC0XrDe0kk0js=","message":"Completed in e1d296b.\n\nNative hello advertises journals=1. doc.update can capture a checksummed recovery delta against a supplied durable vector in the same transaction as the edit, including previously missing compute-side dependencies. It rejects missing prior durable history and incomplete incoming graphs. Native replies are incremental after the first baseline; deletion-only updates remain recoverable even when state vectors do not advance.\n\nThe Rust hosted gateway supplies its private durable vector, stores each delta before acknowledgement, and returns a small durableUpdate receipt. Private RPC IDs cannot collide with browser IDs or leak late recovery payloads. Browser synchronization sends a barrier when durableUpdates is advertised even if compute already has every edit: failed durable writes cannot be mistaken for synchronization on reconnect. Ordinary direct native clients retain their existing behavior.\n\nDurable Object journals use checksummed 40 KiB chunks, compact at 64 segments / 256 KiB tail payload, reuse bounded chunk slots and materialize a graph only for compaction/export. Journals and explicit Save snapshots remain separate but share 512 records / 64 MiB raw recovery payload. Small authoritative usage counters avoid rewriting the whole ID registry per edit; the layout marker prevents missing counters from silently resetting quotas. Manifests, payloads, registry admission and accounting commit atomically. FIFO recovery operations cap queued requests at 256 and encoded update payload at 8 MiB. Internal owner-checked journal listing/export is available to the trusted restore control plane; public routes and browser doc.restore remain denied.\n\nValidation: 46 native tests, core-only cargo check, 12 Worker integration tests and 38 browser-session tests passed. The new real-socket/native tests cover baselines, small subsequent deltas, deletion-only edits, outside edits, corrupt vectors/checksums, missing dependencies and repair. The real workerd test covers concurrent authors, 80 dependent queued updates, 140 additional edits and repeated compaction, retries, spoofed recovery fields, a deterministic paused upstream to exercise byte admission, ownership, shared quota rejection without mutation, object restart and SIGKILL plus destruction of compute files/state followed by restoring a journal into fresh compute. Source generations remain separate.\n\nBoth full-WASM flows passed: hosted GitHub/repository opening, confirming the complete typed text was recoverable before Save, durable Save and reload; and two-editor collaboration/undo/interrupted Save/restart/reload/context fencing/reopen. Logs: bin/journal-{native-all,core-check,cloud-all,browser-tests,hosted-browser}.log and bin/web/durable-updates-{build,collaboration}.log. Documentation links and git diff --check pass.\n\nRebuilt/staged the tested WASM bundle to bin/web/rayde, rebuilt the native session and restarted the existing local service with its URL, workspace, token and environment preserved. The live service advertises journals=1 and serves the tested adapter. Hosted durability was tested through local workerd; the direct local session does not itself become a Durable Object. No push/deployment.\n\nKeep the parent tracks open. This protects acknowledged browser edits and dependencies captured at their barriers. Full filesystem/tombstone authority, changes after the last barrier, automatic compute restoration, stable document IDs, leases/fencing, retention/hibernation and the production MSB provider remain separate work. A compute graph missing prior durable history must be restored by the control plane before further journalled edits.","files":null}]}